1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:05,811
Percentage increases, decreases and quick estimation. Rules for everyday bills.

2
00:00:06,273 --> 00:00:08,973
Suppose two school groups are selling tickets for the

3
00:00:08,973 --> 00:00:12,033
annual function. Group A sold 225 tickets out of

4
00:00:12,033 --> 00:00:15,413
300. Group B sold 200 tickets out of 250.

5
00:00:15,413 --> 00:00:20,439
The two totals are different, so the numbers 225

6
00:00:20,439 --> 00:00:25,333
and 200 cannot be compared directly. Who actually did

7
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:29,812
better? A percentage puts different totals onto one common

8
00:00:29,812 --> 00:00:32,824
scale, and that scale is always out of one hundred.

9
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:35,693
Now let us convert both groups to one common

10
00:00:35,693 --> 00:00:38,496
scale. Both 225 out of 300 and 200 out

11
00:00:38,496 --> 00:00:42,573
of 250 can be written as fractions. 225 out

12
00:00:42,573 --> 00:00:46,693
of 300 is 3 over 4, which is 75

13
00:00:46,693 --> 00:00:49,985
out of 100, so that is 75 percent. 200

14
00:00:49,985 --> 00:00:53,313
out of 250 is 4 over 5, which is

15
00:00:53,313 --> 00:00:56,027
80 out of 100, so that is 80 percent.

16
00:00:56,027 --> 00:00:59,251
So group B is closer to the target. The

17
00:00:59,251 --> 00:01:04,453
rule is: percentage equals part divided by whole, multiplied

18
00:01:04,453 --> 00:01:07,807
by one hundred. And always name the whole, because

19
00:01:07,807 --> 00:01:11,104
the percentage is measured from that reference.

20
00:01:11,693 --> 00:01:14,648
Now think about a change. Suppose a value grows

21
00:01:14,648 --> 00:01:18,061
by 35 percent. Start with the original value drawn

22
00:01:18,061 --> 00:01:21,958
as a full bar of one hundred percent. A

23
00:01:21,958 --> 00:01:25,598
35 percent increase attaches one more block to that

24
00:01:25,598 --> 00:01:29,345
bar. The original hundred percent and the new thirty

25
00:01:29,345 --> 00:01:32,705
five percent together make one hundred and thirty five

26
00:01:32,705 --> 00:01:34,645
percent. So a 35 percent increase means the new

27
00:01:34,645 --> 00:01:37,913
value is 135 percent of the original. The increase

28
00:01:37,913 --> 00:01:41,684
is always measured from the original, never from the new value.

29
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:46,312
Let us apply that to money. Last month the

30
00:01:46,312 --> 00:01:51,500
sales were Rs 98,700. This month they are up

31
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:56,940
by 35 percent. First find the increase: 35 over

32
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:03,580
100 times 98,700 is Rs 34,545. Then add it

33
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:08,468
to the original: 98,700 plus 34,545 gives Rs 1,33,245.

34
00:02:08,468 --> 00:02:14,940
Here is the shortcut: 135 percent of 98,700 is

35
00:02:14,940 --> 00:02:19,326
the same Rs 1,33,245, because the new value is

36
00:02:19,326 --> 00:02:22,171
135 percent of the old one.

37
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:25,549
Now the other direction. A price tag shows Rs

38
00:02:25,549 --> 00:02:28,111
550, and the shop offers 10 percent off. Ten

39
00:02:28,111 --> 00:02:31,660
percent of 550 is Rs 55. Take it away:

40
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:35,291
550 minus 55 is Rs 495, and that is

41
00:02:35,291 --> 00:02:38,940
what you pay. Now compare the two changes on

42
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:42,640
one panel. An increase of p percent gives 100

43
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:46,400
percent plus p percent. A decrease of p percent

44
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:49,968
gives 100 percent minus p percent, so 10 percent

45
00:02:49,968 --> 00:02:51,471
off leaves 90 percent.

46
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:56,120
Here is the mental toolkit. Ten percent is one

47
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:59,083
tenth, so move the decimal point one place. Five

48
00:02:59,083 --> 00:03:03,215
percent is half of ten percent. Fifteen percent is

49
00:03:03,215 --> 00:03:06,560
ten percent plus five percent, and twenty percent is

50
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:09,747
simply two tens. A common mistake is to say

51
00:03:09,747 --> 00:03:14,183
that five percent is half of twenty percent; the

52
00:03:14,183 --> 00:03:16,560
cross shows that this is wrong. Now use 480:

53
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:20,000
ten percent is 48, five percent is 24, so

54
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,611
fifteen percent is 72. Build hard percentages out of ten percent.

55
00:03:25,053 --> 00:03:27,528
Now use that on a bill. The bill is

56
00:03:27,528 --> 00:03:32,113
Rs 477.80, which is awkward, so round it to

57
00:03:32,113 --> 00:03:35,417
Rs 480. Ten percent of 480 is 48. Five

58
00:03:35,417 --> 00:03:40,581
percent is half of that, so 24, and fifteen

59
00:03:40,581 --> 00:03:43,168
percent is 48 plus 24, that is 72. Subtract

60
00:03:43,168 --> 00:03:46,687
that from the rounded bill: 480 minus 72 is

61
00:03:46,687 --> 00:03:49,477
408. So you pay about Rs 410, and the

62
00:03:49,477 --> 00:03:55,364
approximation sign stays on the board, because this is an estimate.

63
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:59,173
Let us put the two lanes side by side.

64
00:03:59,173 --> 00:04:03,460
The exact lane works with the real bill: fifteen

65
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:06,080
percent of 477.80 is Rs 71.67, and 477.80 minus

66
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:10,289
71.67 is 406.13. The mental lane rounded the bill

67
00:04:10,289 --> 00:04:14,900
to 480 and used 72, which gave about 408,

68
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:17,540
or about 410. The two answers are close, so

69
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:23,460
the estimate is sensible. Remember: an estimate is a

70
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:29,520
reasonableness check, not exact arithmetic. The exact lane is

71
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:33,091
only there to show that the quick answer is believable.

72
00:04:33,690 --> 00:04:36,300
So here is the decision map. To convert, divide

73
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:39,154
the part by the whole and multiply by one

74
00:04:39,154 --> 00:04:42,330
hundred. To handle an increase, add the percentage to

75
00:04:42,330 --> 00:04:45,190
one hundred. To handle a decrease, subtract it from

76
00:04:45,190 --> 00:04:49,560
one hundred. To estimate, round the number first, and

77
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:53,330
then build the percentage from ten percent. Four moves:

78
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:56,321
convert, change, estimate, and check.

79
00:04:56,790 --> 00:05:01,190
Your turn. Four quick checks. A: convert 18 out

80
00:05:01,190 --> 00:05:05,290
of 24 into a percentage. B: find the price

81
00:05:05,290 --> 00:05:09,170
of an item marked Rs 800 with 10 percent

82
00:05:09,170 --> 00:05:12,825
off. C: estimate 20 percent of Rs 357.30 by

83
00:05:12,825 --> 00:05:17,630
first rounding it to 360. D: increase Rs 900

84
00:05:17,630 --> 00:05:21,110
by 35 percent. Now pause the video, work all

85
00:05:21,110 --> 00:05:26,001
four out on paper, and then we will check every answer.

86
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:40,957
Let us check every answer. A: 18 out of

87
00:05:40,957 --> 00:05:45,203
24 is three quarters, so it is 75 percent.

88
00:05:45,203 --> 00:05:48,057
B: ten percent of 800 is 80, so 800

89
00:05:48,057 --> 00:05:52,000
minus 80 is Rs 720. C: twenty percent of

90
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,743
360 is two tens of 36, which is 36

91
00:05:54,743 --> 00:05:58,990
plus 36, so it is about Rs 72. D:

92
00:05:58,990 --> 00:06:03,130
135 percent of 900 is Rs 1,215, because 900

93
00:06:03,130 --> 00:06:08,224
plus 315 gives 1,215. Every answer used the whole

94
00:06:08,224 --> 00:06:09,181
as its base.

95
00:06:09,837 --> 00:06:13,097
Let us put it together. One: compare, because a

96
00:06:13,097 --> 00:06:16,597
percentage is always out of one hundred. Two: change,

97
00:06:16,597 --> 00:06:19,097
where an increase adds to one hundred percent and

98
00:06:19,097 --> 00:06:23,497
a decrease subtracts from it. Three: estimate, by rounding

99
00:06:23,497 --> 00:06:26,457
first and then building the percentage from ten percent.

100
00:06:26,457 --> 00:06:30,626
Four: check, because an estimate is a reasonableness check

101
00:06:30,626 --> 00:06:36,677
and not exact arithmetic. Compare, change, estimate, check. Thank

102
00:06:36,677 --> 00:06:39,028
you for watching, and see you in the next lesson.
